|~ `::boolean` and what is declared under it: its constructor and its
   refusal, and the words that run on a boolean, the negation and the
   two that join booleans.
   The decisions it exists for: [D114]. ~|

:boolean
  |~~ A boolean is `true` or `false`, the answer to a question about a
      value. A condition is a boolean and nothing else is truthy:
      `filter` and `every` take code that answers one, `if` takes one
      computed at the call, and any other value is refused there.

      It is written `true` or `false`, as JSON writes it, and ranks
      after null and before every number. The words that ask answer one,
      `eq`, `gt`, `has`, `contains`, `is`; `not` turns it over, `and`
      and `or` join two, and `allOf` and `anyOf` join the conditions of
      one value, `5 | allOf (gt 0) (lt 10)` answering `true`. After
      steps, `false !| true` answers whether they failed.
      ::snippet~(::boolean | manifest) lists its words and refusals.
      ::link~(::any/if | doc) ::link~(::qlang/values | doc)
  ~~|

:boolean/isABoolean
  |~~ True is a boolean. ~~|
  ::law~(true | type | eq ::boolean)

:boolean/buildsABoolean
  |~~ The constructor of a boolean answers it. ~~|
  ::law~(::boolean(false) | eq false)

:boolean/construct
  |~~ Reads a boolean as itself. ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:boolean/PayloadNotBooleanError
  |~~ `::boolean` reads its payload as the boolean it is; a payload of
      another kind is refused.
  ~~|

:boolean/PayloadNotBooleanError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no boolean and is refused. ~~|
  ::law~(::boolean(1) !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::boolean/PayloadNotBooleanError ::number])

:boolean/not
  |~~ Answers the other boolean, `true | not` answering `false`, and so
      turns the condition before it, `filter ~(gt 1 | not)`. The subject
      is a boolean, and `not` asks nothing of a value of another kind:
      null, zero and an empty vector are each refused, so a value is
      asked whether it is null by `eq null`, and a container whether it
      is empty by `empty`. A condition is asked first and turned after,
      `eq 5 | not`, or asked at once by `ne 5`; given to `not` as a
      value, `5 | not (eq 5)`, it leaves `not` a subject that is no
      boolean, and is refused.
      ::link~(::any/ne | doc) ::link~(::vec/empty | doc)
      ::link~(::boolean/and | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:boolean/not/answer
  |~~ The other boolean: `false` for `true`, `true` for `false`. ~~|
  ::boolean

:boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError
  |~~ `not` turns a boolean: a condition is asked first and turned after
      it, `eq 5 | not`, or asked at once by `ne 5`; a value is asked
      whether it is null by `eq null`, and a container whether it is
      empty by `empty`.
  ~~|

:boolean/not/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `not` takes no value: it answers the opposite of the boolean in the
      pipe, and a condition goes before it as a step, `5 | eq 5 | not`.
  ~~|

:boolean/not/turnsTrueFalse
  |~~ Not true is false. ~~|
  ::law~(true | not | eq false)

:boolean/not/turnsFalseTrue
  |~~ Not false is true. ~~|
  ::law~(false | not)

:boolean/not/turnsTheConditionBeforeIt
  |~~ A condition is turned after it is asked. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2] | filter ~(gt 1 | not) | eq [1])

:boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesNull
  |~~ Null is no boolean; it is asked for by `eq null`. ~~|
  ::law~(null | not !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError ::null])

:boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A number is no boolean, and `not` refuses it. ~~|
  ::law~(0 | not !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError ::number])

:boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesAConditionGivenAsAValue
  |~~ A condition given to `not` leaves it a subject that is no boolean. ~~|
  ::law~(5 | not (eq 5) !| type | eq ::boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError)

:boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesAnEmptyVector
  |~~ An empty vector is no boolean. ~~|
  ::law~([] | not !| type | eq ::boolean/not/SubjectNotBooleanError)

:boolean/not/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesAConditionGiven
  |~~ A condition given to the call is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(true | not (eq 5) !| type | eq ::boolean/not/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:boolean/and
  |~~ Answers whether the subject and the boolean the call gives both
      hold, `true | and false` answering `false`. Both are booleans: a
      subject of another kind is refused under the word, and a right of
      another kind under the part. The right is computed against the
      subject, the boolean the left answered, so it reaches neither the
      element nor the record the left was asked of: in `filter ~(gt 1 |
      and (lt 3))` the `lt` is asked of a boolean and is refused. The
      conditions of one value hold together by `allOf`, `filter ~(allOf
      (gt 1) (lt 3))`, and of one record the same way, `allOf /active
      (get /age | gte 18)`, three conditions or more among them. `or`
      answers whether one of the two holds.
      ::link~(::any/allOf | doc) ::link~(::boolean/or | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:boolean/and/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject and the right both hold, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:boolean/and/right
  |~~ The boolean joined with the subject, computed against the subject. ~~|
  /0

:boolean/and/right/NotBooleanError
  |~~ The right is a boolean, `true | and (5 | gt 3)`; the conditions of
      one value hold together by `allOf`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::boolean)

:boolean/and/SubjectNotBooleanError
  |~~ `and` joins two booleans, the subject and the right: the
      conditions of one value hold together by `allOf (gt 1) (lt 3)`, and
      of one record by `allOf (get /active) (get /age | gte 18)`.
  ~~|

:boolean/and/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `and` joins the subject with one boolean: three conditions or more
      hold together by `allOf`, `allOf (gt 1) (lt 9) (ne 5)`.
  ~~|

:boolean/and/holdsWhenBothHold
  |~~ True and true is true. ~~|
  ::law~(true | and true)

:boolean/and/isFalseWhenOneIsFalse
  |~~ True and false is false. ~~|
  ::law~(true | and false | not)

:boolean/and/computesTheRightAgainstTheSubject
  |~~ The right is computed against the boolean on the left. ~~|
  ::law~(true | and (eq true))

:boolean/and/right/NotBooleanError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A right that is no boolean is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(true | and 1 !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::boolean/and/right/NotBooleanError ::number])

:boolean/and/SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A subject that is no boolean is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | and true !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::boolean/and/SubjectNotBooleanError ::number])

:boolean/and/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoBooleans
  |~~ A third condition is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(true | and true true !| type | eq ::boolean/and/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:boolean/and/asksTheRightOfTheBoolean
  |~~ The right is asked of the boolean the left answered, so a comparison of the element there is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1 | and (lt 3)) !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::call/KindAnswersNoVerbError ::boolean])

:boolean/or
  |~~ Answers whether the subject or the boolean the call gives holds,
      `false | or true` answering `true`, and `false` when neither does.
      Both are booleans: a subject of another kind is refused under the
      word, and a right of another kind under the part. The right is
      computed against the subject, the boolean the left answered, as
      for `and`, so the conditions of one value hold by one of them
      through `anyOf`, `5 | anyOf (eq 5) (eq 10)`, and of one record the
      same way, `anyOf (get /role | eq "admin") (get /role | eq "owner")`, three
      conditions or more among them.
      ::link~(::any/anyOf | doc) ::link~(::boolean/and | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:boolean/or/answer
  |~~ `true` when the subject or the right holds, `false` otherwise. ~~|
  ::boolean

:boolean/or/right
  |~~ The boolean joined with the subject, computed against the subject. ~~|
  /0

:boolean/or/right/NotBooleanError
  |~~ The right is a boolean, `false | or (5 | gt 3)`; the conditions of
      one value hold together by `anyOf`.
  ~~|
  ::holds~(is ::boolean)

:boolean/or/SubjectNotBooleanError
  |~~ `or` joins two booleans, the subject and the right: the conditions
      of one value hold by one of them through `anyOf (eq 5) (eq 10)`,
      and of one record by `anyOf (/role | eq "admin") (/role | eq
      "owner")`.
  ~~|

:boolean/or/ArgsBeyondPartsError
  |~~ `or` joins the subject with one boolean: three conditions or more
      hold by one of them through `anyOf`, `anyOf (eq 5) (eq 10) (eq 15)`.
  ~~|

:boolean/or/isTrueWhenOneIsTrue
  |~~ False or true is true. ~~|
  ::law~(false | or true)

:boolean/or/isFalseWhenNeitherHolds
  |~~ False or false is false. ~~|
  ::law~(false | or false | not)

:boolean/or/computesTheRightAgainstTheSubject
  |~~ The right is computed against the boolean on the left. ~~|
  ::law~(false | or (not))

:boolean/or/right/NotBooleanError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A right that is no boolean is refused under the part. ~~|
  ::law~(false | or 1 !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::boolean/or/right/NotBooleanError ::number])

:boolean/or/SubjectNotBooleanError/refusesANumber
  |~~ A subject that is no boolean is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(1 | or true !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::boolean/or/SubjectNotBooleanError ::number])

:boolean/or/ArgsBeyondPartsError/refusesTwoBooleans
  |~~ A third condition is refused under the word. ~~|
  ::law~(false | or true true !| type | eq ::boolean/or/ArgsBeyondPartsError)

:boolean/or/asksTheRightOfTheBoolean
  |~~ The right is asked of the boolean the left answered, so a comparison of the element there is refused. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | filter ~(gt 1 | or (lt 3)) !| [type (get /actualType)] | eq [::call/KindAnswersNoVerbError ::boolean])

:boolean/toString
  |~~ Answers the literal of the boolean as a string, `true | toString`
      answering `"true"`.
      ::link~(::string/toString | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:boolean/toString/answer
  |~~ `"true"` or `"false"`. ~~|
  ::string

:boolean/toString/writesTheLiteral
  |~~ A boolean answers its literal. ~~|
  ::law~([true false] | map ~toString | eq ["true" "false"])

:boolean/toKeyword
  |~~ Answers the keyword of the boolean's literal, `true | toKeyword`
      answering `:true`, as `groupBy` files the elements a condition
      parts, `[1 2 3] | groupBy ~(gt 1)`.
      ::link~(::vec/groupBy | doc)
  ~~|
  ::builtin{}

:boolean/toKeyword/answer
  |~~ `:true` or `:false`. ~~|
  ::keyword

:boolean/toKeyword/readsTheKeyGroupByFiledItUnder
  |~~ A boolean turns into the key `groupBy` files a condition under. ~~|
  ::law~([1 2 3] | groupBy ~(gt 1) | query (true | toKeyword) | eq [2 3])
